A||N||9||Eric_PA||What GA Brakes look like after about a year||||1||
Z||000000||Eric_PA||04-05-2001||11:19 PM||||Well I set out to redo my front end and while doing so I decided to clean the GA brakes that I installed last summer. As you can see below, they rust very fast so I took them off and cleaned them up and painted them.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Mar27$12.jpg"> <P>And here they are painted  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Mar27$15.jpg"> <BR>Also I forgot to mention that I installed those Russel Speed Bleeders.. They are great and would recomend them to anyone.<P>Eric<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||WKDFIRO||04-05-2001||11:55 PM||||Holy cow!<P>Ive never seen calipers rusted that badly unless it was sitting in a field somewhere! <P>Wha happen???<P>Should they be painted prior to instalation?<P>The GA conversion, is that using the same rotor or is it bigger? Or just vented?<P>They look good now!||||reg||14
Z||000002||Eric_PA||04-06-2001||12:06 AM||||WKDFIRO... I would recommend painting these calipers if you install them. From what I understand they are cast calipers and rust very fast if not protected with something. These brakes only have about 5000 miles on them. The car was off the road all winter long too. <BR>That is a new vented rotor. I believe that it is about the same size as the factory one. I bought this setup from the Fiero Store last year. Actually it is from a 91 Beretta, but better known as the GA upgrade. Overall the car stops a bit better and I don't get brake fade like I use to. I just hope that the red paint doesn't make it look ricey at all.<P>Eric<P>||||reg||1
